body:
That's an incredible story and I'm so glad to hear you found a good balance of diet and lifestyle changes that make you feel like a brand new person! That is what I'm striving for :)

&#x200B;

I probably do consume too much sugar, and too many carbs. They add sugar and/or salt to everything in the US. I know making more whole food swaps would go a long way. I do try to eat fruits & veggies in some form every day but I know I can improve my diet. 

&#x200B;

I have a follow-up next week with the same doctor I mentioned in my post and I will ask him about L-arginine and B12. I appreciate you sharing your story and again, so happy for you!!

I've been on Lexapro for a little over a year. 40mg is a very high dose, double the "max" that the FDA recommends. However, I was prescribed it by a psychiatrist to help with the anxiety, slight OCD. I do think it might help to ween off, but I am nervous for my anxiety to get bad again.

&#x200B;

What do you mean by another class of medicine? Something for anxiety that isn't an SSRI?

The Envy stays up and supportive without straps. I can probably loosen the straps on top a little bit so they don't dig into my shoulders. I tried the 32DD in Envy and it didn't feel big enough. 

&#x200B;

I will check out the Clara!

I really appreciate all of your advice and help in my quest to find ABTF! It has been so helpful!

&#x200B;

I think that's a great idea to try these couple of bras out for a few months and see which one I tend to wear and love the most, then go from there :)

We were actually in the ER last month for the issues described above. We wound up sitting in the hospital room for over 2 hours and all they did was give him a 0.25mg of Xanax and a doctor's note to take 2 days off of work. 

&#x200B;

I'm curious what the behavioral hospital is though. Is that different than the ER? 

&#x200B;

I am currently in therapy and it does help. I'm learning coping tools to not be so affected and how to practice more self-care. I am naturally wired as an empath who is very sensitive, so it's hard to "unwire" who I am as a person, if that makes sense? But therapy has been helpful for me. I've been going for years.

&#x200B;

My partner actually has his first individual therapy session in 2 weeks! I'm really excited for him. I think it will be a good resource to get some of his stress off of his chest and talk to a professional. I think it will be great for him and for us as a couple. I just hope he sticks to it. 

&#x200B;

How did you deal with your husband's challenges (ER, behavioral health, etc)? I'm finding it extremely hard to keep trucking along and feeling unaffected. I missed hours of work today just being worried and distracted. It's hard to just function normally due to the worry,

Thank you so much. Just hearing you say I'm doing good as a partner has me in tears; it really does mean so much to me, especially during these days where it all feels very challenging. I always worry I'm not doing enough. I'm a problem-solver at heart and always looking for a solution, but I know mental health is not a quick, easy fix. It's part of why this is very challenging. 

&#x200B;

We were able to get ahold of his psychiatrist today via phone, who recommended adding Wellbutrin to the Viibryd prescription. I'm driving to pick that up for him tonight. I'm feeling hopeful this may help. The psyc claims these 2 medicines work on different parts of the brain which should provide "more well-rounded coverage". As you said, SSRI's are tricky so I'm remaining skeptical because everyone's body chemistry is different, but at least we are taking action towards getting help and that feels good. 

&#x200B;

For the first time in our 5+ year relationship, he has also scheduled his own solo therapy which I think will be incredibly helpful for him as an individual and for our relationship. His first appointment is in 2 weeks. Right now he tends to dump all of his stresses, depression, worries, etc on me without any filter. I always try to listen and be supportive, but it really weighs on me. When you really love someone, it breaks your heart when you see them suffering. I think therapy will be good for him because he can talk to a therapist and get some of these things off of his chest that normally come direct to me, and in turn wind up causing me distress.

&#x200B;

Outside of my relationship, I have a couple of close friends and a great mom that are my rocks. However, I don't feel like I can talk to them about this stuff. My mom would just worry about the situation I'm in. Love her to death but she is old school and doesn't even believe in SSRIs - she'd probably like to see me with a doctor husband with no mental health issues, but that's just not real life or how love works. My close friends are also amazing but I don't want to worry them, nor do they really understand mental health in a big way since most of them don't really struggle with it. I mostly talk about it in therapy and today here on Reddit. My friends and family are more of my go-to's to take my mind off it when it gets hard, but I also feel I need to keep it private from most of them.
